      timeline: [
        '1700s: First documented in Italian and French sources as a fishing card game.',
        '1800s: Spread through Europe; documented in English by Hoyle.',
        '1900s: Common in American households; many regional variants develop.',
        "Present: Widely taught as a children's and family game; many national variants (Royal Cassino, Draw Cassino, etc.).",
      ],
      evolution: "The building mechanicâ€”combining table cards into a 'build' that can only be taken with the matching rankâ€”is Cassino's most sophisticated feature, unique among fishing games.",
      cultural: 'A game taught to children as an introduction to arithmetic and card handling. Remains popular in Scandinavia, the Caribbean, and North America.',
